; pi4DmaProbe.pi4 - STAGE 1 of getting pixels moved by hardware.
;
; It answers one question with a number: IS THE SoC'S 2D DMA BLITTER
; FASTER THAN THE CPU AT FILLING THIS SURFACE, AND BY HOW MUCH?
;
; The number to beat was measured on this board on 2026-08-26 by
; RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/NotBuilding/pi4NeonLiar.pi4:
;
;                           MMU off      MMU on, DRAM cacheable
;       clear, stores only  356.9 ms          266.4 ms
;
; against a 25 ms frame budget. This program runs THE SAME FILL through
; display.pi4's CPU loop and through RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4's
; hardware path, in one run, back to back, on the same surface, and
; prints both. Nothing here is compared across sessions.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4\Examples\Diagnostics\pi4DmaProbe.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o _work\dmaprobe.img
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship. It adds nothing under Lib/ and changes
; nothing that does.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT TO RUN, AND WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS
; ======================================================================
; Reset the board, bring the screen up fresh, then run this. Anvil will
; stamp two or three lines of its own over the picture when the payload
; returns - that is Anvil, not this program, and pi4NeonLiar.pi4's
; header explains why nothing here can prevent it.
;
; THE SECTIONS COME IN THE ORDER THAT MAKES A FAILURE READABLE, and the
; order is the whole design of this program: nothing is aimed at the
; framebuffer until every property it depends on has been proved in a
; 64 KiB sandbox.
;
;   1. THE CONTROLLER. What the ENABLE register says, which channels
;      the device tree offers, which channels the FIRMWARE offers, and
;      whether the chosen channel is a full engine or a LITE one.
;
;      IF THE PAGE FIELD IS NOT ZERO, stop. Every address this program
;      computes is wrong by one or more gigabytes and nothing below is
;      meaningful. See dma.pi4's HAZARD 1.
;
;   2. THE YLENGTH CALIBRATION. The one thing the vendor PDF leaves
;      ambiguous for this engine. The probe writes nothing; it counts
;      rows out of the engine's own SOURCE_AD register.
;
;      IF THE ADVANCE IS 4, YLENGTH is a plain row count.
;      IF IT IS 8, YLENGTH is rows minus one.
;      IF IT IS 65540, TDMODE was ignored and the channel is behaving
;        like a LITE engine, which should be impossible on 0/2/4/5/6.
;      WRITE THE ANSWER DOWN. From then on a program can state it with
;      DmaSetYLengthBias() and skip the probe entirely.
;
;   3. CORRECTNESS, IN A SANDBOX. Six tests over 64 KiB of scratch, all
;      verified byte by byte, all with the framebuffer untouched:
;      the fill lands where it should and NOWHERE ELSE (which is the
;      independent cross-check on the calibration), the stride skips
;      the right gap, a rectangle copy is exact, an overlapping
;      downward copy - the scroll case - is exact, and the two
;      refusals that protect the board actually refuse.
;
;      IF ANY OF THESE FAIL, the timings below are meaningless and the
;      screen section is skipped. Read the failing test's own line: it
;      says which byte, what was there and what should have been.
;
;   4. THE TIMINGS. The fill, the blit, and the present, each done both
;      ways on the same bytes.
;
;   4d. THE DISPLAY PATH ITSELF. Added 2026-08-27, when display.pi4
;      gained an accelerated DisplayFillRect behind #DSP_DMA_LINKED.
;      The same rectangle is drawn twice - engine off, then engine on -
;      and the two are compared BYTE FOR BYTE over a window eight pixels
;      larger on every side, so damage beside the rectangle counts.
;
;      IT ALSO ASKS DisplayDmaOps() WHETHER THE ENGINE RAN AT ALL, and
;      that check comes first. Every accelerated primitive in
;      display.pi4 falls back to the CPU when anything is uncertain, and
;      a fallback draws the RIGHT PICTURE - so a green comparison with a
;      zero transfer count means this section compared the CPU path
;      against itself and proved nothing.
;
;      IT RUNS AFTER EVERY TIMING, because 4a and 4b use DisplayFillRect
;      and DisplayBlit as the CPU reference and would be meaningless if
;      those had gone through the engine. DisplayUseDma() is off by
;      default and 4d is the first thing here that turns it on.
;
;   5. THE PICTURE. Colour bars drawn entirely by the DMA engine, with
;      one line of text from display.pi4 so the two can be seen
;      cohabiting. If the bars are there and are the right colours, the
;      hardware path is not merely fast, it is right.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE THREE THINGS THAT WOULD MAKE THIS LIE
; ======================================================================
;
;   * MEASURING THE CACHE FLUSH AS PART OF THE CPU'S FILL. display.pi4
;     auto-flushes after every primitive (display.pi4:2337-2343), which
;     is one "dc cvac" per 64 bytes plus a barrier over the whole
;     surface - about 130,000 cache maintenance operations that have
;     nothing to do with how fast a store loop is. THE AUTO-FLUSH IS
;     TURNED OFF around the timed CPU pass, exactly as pi4NeonLiar.pi4
;     does at :1978, so that 266.4 ms is being compared with 266.4 ms.
;
;   * NOT MEASURING THE CACHE CLEAN AS PART OF THE DMA'S PRESENT. The
;     mirror image, and it is the dishonest direction. A blit from a
;     CPU-composed shadow buffer must clean that buffer out of the
;     D-cache before the engine reads it, and with the MMU on that is
;     129,600 lines over 8.29 MB. IT IS TIMED AND PRINTED SEPARATELY
;     AND THEN ADDED IN, because a present that quotes only the DMA
;     time is quoting half the bill. The FILL has no such cost - its
;     source is thirty-two bytes - which is exactly why the fill is
;     the honest headline and the present is not.
;
;   * A SURFACE THAT IS NOT 1920 x 1080. This board reports 1824 x 984
;     after overscan on at least one configuration (damage.pi4's
;     header records it), so every derived figure here is computed from
;     DisplayWidth(), DisplayHeight() and DisplayPitch() as the
;     firmware reported them, and the actual geometry is printed first.
;     Do not compare a rate against a remembered one without checking
;     that line.
;
; ======================================================================
; MEMORY THIS PROGRAM USES, AND WHY EACH ADDRESS
; ======================================================================
;   $400000   this image. --load-addr.
;   $480000   its BSS, 512 KiB above the load address
;             (#A64_BSS_GAP in RaspberryPi4/A64Assembler.pbi).
;   $600000   the MMU tables, 8 KiB, when #DP_MMU is 1. The same place
;             pi4NeonLiar.pi4 puts them.
;   $700000   the DMA scratch block, 512 bytes. 256-byte aligned and in
;             the low gigabyte, which is what DmaSetScratch() insists
;             on. It was 256 until 2026-08-26; the fill pattern inside
;             it grew from 32 bytes to 256 when a bench run showed the
;             engine reading past the end of it on a long row.
;   $780000   the 64 KiB correctness sandbox. Everything in section 3
;             happens in here and nowhere else.
;   $1000000  the 8.29 MB shadow surface for the blit and present
;             timings. Sixteen megabytes up, so it is clear of the
;             image, clear of the sandbox, and far below the stack top
;             at $3000000.
;
; EVERY ONE OF THOSE IS BELOW $40000000, which is the only address rule
; the legacy DMA engines have and the one that fails silently.
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/memory.pi4's map (:200-215) is where the windows
; come from; the low payload window is $00400000..$07EFFFFF, so the
; shadow at $1000000 plus 8.29 MB ends around $18D0000 and is well
; inside it.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DMA SEAM IS ON IN THIS PROGRAM, AND dma.pi4 THEREFORE MOVES ABOVE
; display.pi4.
;
; #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1 makes display.pi4 compile its accelerated fill,
; copy and scroll (display.pi4's THE DMA SEAM section). Those call
; DmaFillPattern32, DmaCopy2D, DmaCopy, DmaCacheRect and DmaSetBounds,
; and definition order is the convention in this dialect - there is not
; one Declare anywhere in RaspberryPi4/Lib/ - so dma.pi4 has to be
; parsed first. It used to sit last in this list, which was harmless
; while nothing in display.pi4 named it.
;
; THE CONSTANT IS MANDATORY AND HAS NO DEFAULT. display.pi4 cannot
; supply one: a default there and a different value here would be a hard
; "Constant already defined" error, so the main file states it and the
; library reads it. Same seam as x509.pico2's #X509_SIG_LINKED.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DSP_DMA_LINKED = 1

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"


; ======================================================================
;  CONFIGURATION
; ======================================================================

; 1 = build the identity map and run with DRAM Normal Write-Back
; Cacheable, which is the configuration the 266.4 ms was measured in
; and therefore the one the comparison has to be made in. 0 = the
; uncached run, which is worth doing once because the DMA engine does
; not care either way and the CPU does: with the caches off the CPU
; number goes to 356.9 ms and the ratio gets FLATTERING. The honest
; headline is the cached one.
#DP_MMU = 1

#DP_TABLES  = $600000
#DP_SCRATCH = $700000
#DP_SCRATCH_BYTES = 512
#DP_SAND    = $780000
#DP_SAND_BYTES = 65536
#DP_SHADOW  = $1000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SECTION 4d's TWO CAPTURE BUFFERS. Added 2026-08-27 with the display
;  path's A/B.
;
;  display.pi4 now routes DisplayFillRect through the engine when
;  #DSP_DMA_LINKED is 1 and DisplayUseDma(1) has been called. That
;  wiring has its own arithmetic - a clip, a pitch, a byte offset - and
;  its own cache sequence, and NONE of it is what pi4DmaGate.pi4 tests:
;  the gate has no framebuffer, no mailbox and no display.pi4 in its
;  build at all. So the wiring has to be proved here, against the CPU
;  path, on the surface it will actually run on.
;
;  TWO BUFFERS OF 64 KiB, one per pass, so the comparison is between two
;  captured images rather than between an image and a remembered
;  expectation. 128 x 128 pixels at 4 bytes is exactly 65536.
;
;  $790000 and $7A0000 sit above the sandbox at $780000 and well below
;  the shadow at $1000000. They are the same two addresses
;  pi4DmaGate.pi4 uses for its OUT and SRC buffers, which is a
;  coincidence of both programs wanting scratch above $780000 and not a
;  shared layout - the two never run at once.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#DP_CAP_A   = $790000
#DP_CAP_B   = $7A0000

; The A/B rectangle, and the window captured around it.
;
; THE WINDOW IS EIGHT PIXELS LARGER ON EVERY SIDE AND THAT MARGIN IS THE
; POINT. A wrong stride, a YLENGTH one too large or a burst that runs
; past the end of a row does not corrupt the rectangle - it corrupts
; what is NEXT to it, and a comparison that only looked at the rectangle
; would pass every one of them. It is the same argument pi4DmaGate.pi4's
; header makes for comparing whole buffers, applied to a surface that is
; far too big to compare whole.
#DP_AB_WINX = 56
#DP_AB_WINY = 56
#DP_AB_WINW = 128
#DP_AB_WINH = 128
#DP_AB_X    = 64
#DP_AB_Y    = 64
#DP_AB_W    = 112
#DP_AB_H    = 112

; ASCII codes used below. No character literals - house rule.
#DP_SPACE = 32
#DP_DOT   = 46
#DP_ZERO  = 48
#DP_ALPHA = 55                 ; the letter A, minus ten
#DP_COLON = 58

Global dpFails.i = 0
Global dpChecks.i = 0
Global dpSurfaceOk.i = 0


; ======================================================================
;  OUTPUT HELPERS
;
;  The same shapes pi4NeonLiar.pi4 uses (:376-425), for the same
;  reasons: integer arithmetic only, because .f is refused outright by
;  this backend and a software binary32 multiply costs 1751
;  instructions (math.pi4:114-125), which is not a price a diagnostic
;  should pay to print "1.40".
; ======================================================================

Procedure PutS(*s)
  UartWriteStr(*s)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutNl()
  PrintNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(#DP_ZERO + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(#DP_ALPHA + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

; A value already multiplied by 100, printed with two decimal places.
Procedure PutFix2(v100.i)
  Define w.i
  Define f.i
  w = v100 / 100
  f = v100 - (w * 100)
  If f < 0
    f = 0 - f
  EndIf
  PrintDec(w)
  UartWrite(#DP_DOT)
  UartWrite(#DP_ZERO + (f / 10))
  UartWrite(#DP_ZERO + (f - ((f / 10) * 10)))
EndProcedure

; Microseconds printed as milliseconds with three places, which is the
; unit the 25 ms decision and the 266.4 ms measurement are stated in.
Procedure PutMs(us.i)
  Define w.i
  Define f.i
  w = us / 1000
  f = us - (w * 1000)
  PrintDec(w)
  UartWrite(#DP_DOT)
  UartWrite(#DP_ZERO + (f / 100))
  UartWrite(#DP_ZERO + ((f / 10) - ((f / 100) * 10)))
  UartWrite(#DP_ZERO + (f - ((f / 10) * 10)))
EndProcedure

Procedure PutLabel(*s)
  UartWriteStr("    ")
  UartWriteStr(*s)
EndProcedure

; One pass/fail line. Every check in this program goes through here, so
; the tally at the end cannot disagree with the lines above it.
Procedure Chk(*name, got.i, want.i)
  dpChecks = dpChecks + 1
  If got = want
    UartWriteStr("  ok    ")
    UartWriteStr(*name)
    PutNl()
  Else
    dpFails = dpFails + 1
    UartWriteStr("  FAIL  ")
    UartWriteStr(*name)
    UartWriteStr("   got ")
    PrintDec(got)
    UartWriteStr(", wanted ")
    PrintDec(want)
    PutNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; The same, when the interesting part is a hex value.
Procedure ChkHex(*name, got.i, want.i)
  dpChecks = dpChecks + 1
  If got = want
    UartWriteStr("  ok    ")
    UartWriteStr(*name)
    PutNl()
  Else
    dpFails = dpFails + 1
    UartWriteStr("  FAIL  ")
    UartWriteStr(*name)
    UartWriteStr("   got $")
    PutHexN(got, 8)
    UartWriteStr(", wanted $")
    PutHexN(want, 8)
    PutNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; When a check fails, say what the library thought was wrong. Reading
; DmaErrorText() is almost always the whole diagnosis.
Procedure PutDmaErr()
  UartWriteStr("        ")
  UartWriteStr(DmaErrorText())
  PutNl()
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 1 - THE CONTROLLER
; ======================================================================

; Print one channel's availability from each of the three sources that
; have an opinion: the device tree, the firmware, and the silicon.
Procedure DpChannelLine(ch.i, dtMask.i, fwMask.i)
  Define en.i
  UartWriteStr("    channel ")
  If ch < 10
    UartWrite(#DP_SPACE)
  EndIf
  PrintDec(ch)
  UartWriteStr("   device tree ")
  If ((dtMask >> ch) & 1) = 1
    UartWriteStr("free ")
  Else
    UartWriteStr("HELD ")
  EndIf
  UartWriteStr("  firmware ")
  If fwMask < 0
    UartWriteStr("?    ")
  Else
    If ((fwMask >> ch) & 1) = 1
      UartWriteStr("free ")
    Else
      UartWriteStr("HELD ")
    EndIf
  EndIf
  en = DmaEnableRaw()
  UartWriteStr("  ENABLE bit ")
  If ((en >> ch) & 1) = 1
    UartWriteStr("1")
  Else
    UartWriteStr("0")
  EndIf
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DpAskFirmware - mailbox tag $00060001, GET_DMA_CHANNELS.
;
; The device tree at Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:106 says $07F5. That is
; what a Linux kernel would be told; it is NOT a promise from the
; firmware that is actually running underneath us right now. The
; firmware will answer the question directly, and when the two disagree
; the firmware is the one to believe - it is the party that would be
; using the channel.
;
; Returns the mask, or -1 if the firmware would not answer. A -1 is not
; a failure of anything: a bare-metal program has no right to expect
; every property tag to exist, and the device-tree mask is a perfectly
; good second source. It is printed as a question mark rather than
; folded into a wrong answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i DpAskFirmware()
  Define m.i
  m = -1
  If MailboxBegin($00060001, 4, 4) <> 0
    MailboxSetWord(0, 0)
    If MailboxSend() <> 0
      m = MailboxWord(0) & $FFFFFFFF
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn m
EndProcedure

Procedure DpReportController()
  Define en.i
  Define page.i
  Define pagelite.i
  Define fw.i

  PutS("-- 1. the DMA controller at $FE007000")
  PutNl()

  en       = DmaEnableRaw()
  page     = (en >> 24) & $F
  pagelite = (en >> 28) & $F

  PutLabel("ENABLE (offset $FF0, so $FE007FF0)   $")
  PutHexN(en, 8)
  PutNl()

  PutLabel("PAGE     (bits 27:24, the 1G window for DMA0-6)   ")
  PrintDec(page)
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("PAGELITE (bits 31:28, the same for DMA7-10)       ")
  PrintDec(pagelite)
  PutNl()

  ; THE CHECK THAT INVALIDATES EVERYTHING BELOW IT IF IT FAILS.
  ; bcm2711-peripherals.txt:4994-5002. A non-zero PAGE means the
  ; engines' one-gigabyte window has been moved, so every bus address
  ; computed from an ARM physical address lands a gigabyte or more away
  ; from where it was meant to. The reset value is zero and dma.pi4
  ; never writes the field.
  Chk("PAGE is zero - the 1G window is where every address here assumes", page, 0)
  If page <> 0
    PutS("        STOP. Nothing below this line means anything. Something on")
    PutNl()
    PutS("        this board has moved the window the 30-bit engines address.")
    PutNl()
  EndIf

  fw = DpAskFirmware()
  PutLabel("firmware GET_DMA_CHANNELS ($00060001)   ")
  If fw < 0
    PutS("no answer - using the device tree only")
  Else
    UartWrite(36)                        ; 36 is the dollar sign
    PutHexN(fw, 4)
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("device tree brcm,dma-channel-mask       $07F5   (bcm2711.dtsi:106)")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  DpChannelLine(0, $07F5, fw)
  DpChannelLine(2, $07F5, fw)
  DpChannelLine(4, $07F5, fw)
  DpChannelLine(5, $07F5, fw)
  DpChannelLine(6, $07F5, fw)
  PutNl()
  PutS("    Channels 1 and 3 are the firmware's, 7-10 are LITE and have no 2D")
  PutNl()
  PutS("    mode at all, 11-14 are DMA4 with a different control block, 15 is")
  PutNl()
  PutS("    the VPU's. Only the five above are candidates.")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 3 - CORRECTNESS, IN A SANDBOX
;
;  Every test in this section writes only inside the 64 KiB at
;  #DP_SAND, and DmaSetBounds() is pointed at exactly that region while
;  they run, so a bug in the rectangle arithmetic here is refused by the
;  library rather than delivered to the framebuffer.
;
;  THE CACHE DISCIPLINE IS EXPLICIT AND IT IS THE CALLER'S. dma.pi4
;  cleans its own control block and its own pattern and nothing else,
;  so every test below cleans what the CPU wrote before the engine
;  reads it and invalidates what the engine wrote before the CPU reads
;  it. With #DP_MMU = 0 those calls are architecturally no-ops and cost
;  a few cycles a line; with #DP_MMU = 1 they are the difference
;  between a test and a coin toss.
; ======================================================================

; Fill a span with a 32-bit word, using the CPU. PokeL, thirty-two bits
; at a time, because a bare Poke is EIGHT bytes on this target and
; would write two words per call.
Procedure DpCpuFill(addr.i, bytes.i, value.i)
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < bytes
    PokeL(addr + i, value & $FFFFFFFF)
    i = i + 4
  Wend
EndProcedure

; Count the 32-bit words in a span that are NOT the expected value.
Procedure.i DpCountBad(addr.i, bytes.i, want.i)
  Define i.i
  Define bad.i
  bad = 0
  i = 0
  While i < bytes
    If (PeekN(addr + i) & $FFFFFFFF) <> (want & $FFFFFFFF)
      bad = bad + 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 4
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn bad
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DpTestFill - the 2D fill, and the independent cross-check on the
; YLENGTH calibration.
;
; A rectangle of 64 bytes on each of 4 rows, at a pitch of 256. Three
; separate properties are checked and they fail in different ways:
;
;   THE RECTANGLE IS FILLED        - the transfer happened at all.
;   THE GAP AFTER EACH ROW IS NOT  - the destination stride is being
;                                    applied, and applied on top of the
;                                    address the row left off at rather
;                                    than instead of it.
;   ROW 4 IS NOT                   - the row count is right. This is
;                                    the cross-check on the bias that
;                                    DmaCalibrate() derived from
;                                    SOURCE_AD, and it is deliberately
;                                    derived a completely different
;                                    way: if the calibration were one
;                                    out, four rows would have been
;                                    asked for and five delivered, and
;                                    this is the check that sees it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DpTestFill()
  Define bad.i
  Define r.i
  Define rowbad.i

  DpCpuFill(#DP_SAND, 8192, 0)
  DmaCacheRange(#DP_SAND, 8192)

  If DmaFillPattern32(#DP_SAND, 256, 64, 4, $A5A5A5A5) = 0
    Chk("2D fill of 4 rows x 64 bytes at pitch 256", 0, 1)
    PutDmaErr()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  DmaCacheRange(#DP_SAND, 8192)

  bad = 0
  r = 0
  While r < 4
    bad = bad + DpCountBad(#DP_SAND + (r * 256), 64, $A5A5A5A5)
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  Chk("the 4 x 64 rectangle is filled                (words wrong)", bad, 0)

  bad = 0
  r = 0
  While r < 4
    bad = bad + DpCountBad((#DP_SAND + (r * 256)) + 64, 192, 0)
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  Chk("the 192-byte gap after each row is untouched  (words wrong)", bad, 0)

  rowbad = DpCountBad(#DP_SAND + (4 * 256), 64, 0)
  Chk("row 4 is untouched - the row count is exact   (words wrong)", rowbad, 0)
  If rowbad <> 0
    PutS("        The YLENGTH bias is one out. DmaYLengthBias() said ")
    PrintDec(DmaYLengthBias())
    PutS(", and a")
    PutNl()
    PutS("        fill therefore wrote one row too many. Nothing below is safe.")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DpTestCopy - a rectangle copy between two non-overlapping regions
; with the same pitch, which is the sprite blit and the present.
;
; The source is filled with a DIFFERENT word per row, not one word
; everywhere. A copy that got the source stride wrong, or that read the
; same row four times, would pass a single-value test and fails this
; one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DpTestCopy()
  Define src.i
  Define dst.i
  Define r.i
  Define bad.i

  src = #DP_SAND + 16384
  dst = #DP_SAND + 24576

  r = 0
  While r < 4
    DpCpuFill(src + (r * 256), 64, $C0DE0000 + r)
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  DpCpuFill(dst, 1024, 0)
  DmaCacheRange(src, 1024)
  DmaCacheRange(dst, 1024)

  If DmaCopy2D(dst, 256, src, 256, 64, 4) = 0
    Chk("2D copy of 4 rows x 64 bytes", 0, 1)
    PutDmaErr()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  DmaCacheRange(dst, 1024)

  bad = 0
  r = 0
  While r < 4
    bad = bad + DpCountBad(dst + (r * 256), 64, $C0DE0000 + r)
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  Chk("every copied row carries its own value        (words wrong)", bad, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DpTestScroll - AN OVERLAPPING DOWNWARD COPY. This is the one test
; here that checks an ASSUMPTION rather than a documented fact.
;
; dma.pi4 allows a copy whose destination starts BELOW its source, on
; the reasoning that SRC_INC and DEST_INC both increment
; (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:3327-3374) so each byte is read before the
; write that would clobber it. The PDF states the increments; it does
; not state that reads and writes retire in address order under
; bursting, and it does not have to for the hardware to be correct.
;
; So it is tested. Eight contiguous 64-byte rows, each holding its own
; index, copied up by one row. If the answer comes back sheared or
; smeared, the library's overlap rule is wrong and the fix is to refuse
; overlap in both directions and let the caller stage through a
; temporary - which costs a second pass over the bytes, which is
; exactly why the assumption was worth making and worth testing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DpTestScroll()
  Define base.i
  Define r.i
  Define bad.i

  base = #DP_SAND + 32768

  r = 0
  While r < 8
    DpCpuFill(base + (r * 64), 64, $5000 + r)
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  DmaCacheRange(base, 512)

  ; Seven rows, from row 1 upward, landing on row 0. Contiguous, so the
  ; pitch equals the row and both strides are zero - and it is still a
  ; 2D transfer, because that is the path a real scroll would take.
  If DmaCopy2D(base, 64, base + 64, 64, 64, 7) = 0
    Chk("overlapping downward copy - the scroll case", 0, 1)
    PutDmaErr()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  DmaCacheRange(base, 512)

  bad = 0
  r = 0
  While r < 7
    bad = bad + DpCountBad(base + (r * 64), 64, $5000 + r + 1)
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  Chk("scroll by one row leaves rows 0..6 = old 1..7 (words wrong)", bad, 0)

  bad = DpCountBad(base + (7 * 64), 64, $5007)
  Chk("row 7, which nothing wrote, is unchanged      (words wrong)", bad, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DpTestRefusals - the checks that protect the board.
;
; None of these touches the hardware. They are here because a guard
; that has never been seen to refuse anything is a guard nobody knows
; is broken - and two of these are the difference between a bad picture
; and a board that needs a power cycle.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DpTestRefusals()
  Chk("DmaAddrOk refuses $40000000, the 1 GiB cap", DmaAddrOk($40000000, 4), 0)
  Chk("DmaAddrOk refuses a buffer that straddles it", DmaAddrOk($3FFFFFF0, 64), 0)
  Chk("DmaAddrOk refuses a zero length", DmaAddrOk(#DP_SAND, 0), 0)
  Chk("DmaAddrOk accepts the sandbox", DmaAddrOk(#DP_SAND, 4096), 1)
  ChkHex("DmaBusAddr($780000) is the uncached alias", DmaBusAddr($780000), $C0780000)

  ; Just below the declared window. One byte outside is outside.
  Chk("a fill below the declared window is refused", DmaFillPattern32(#DP_SAND - 64, 256, 64, 1, 0), 0)
  Chk("...and the reason given is #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS", DmaError(), #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS)

  ; Just past the end of it.
  Chk("a fill past the end of the window is refused", DmaFillPattern32((#DP_SAND + #DP_SAND_BYTES) - 32, 256, 64, 1, 0), 0)
  Chk("...and the reason given is #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS", DmaError(), #DMA_ERR_BOUNDS)

  ; A failed DmaSetChannel() does not change the channel and does not
  ; clear the ready flag, so these two are safe to run in the middle of
  ; a session. That property is worth knowing and is why they are here
  ; rather than at the end.
  Chk("channel 7 is refused - it is a LITE engine", DmaSetChannel(7), 0)
  Chk("...and the reason given is #DMA_ERR_LITE", DmaError(), #DMA_ERR_LITE)
  Chk("channel 1 is refused - it is the firmware's", DmaSetChannel(1), 0)
  Chk("...and the reason given is #DMA_ERR_CHANNEL", DmaError(), #DMA_ERR_CHANNEL)
  Chk("channel 12 is refused - it is DMA4", DmaSetChannel(12), 0)
  Chk("the refusals did not steal the channel", DmaChannel(), 0)
  Chk("the refusals did not clear the ready flag", DmaReady(), 1)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 4 - THE TIMINGS
; ======================================================================

; One measurement, reported with everything it was derived from beside
; it, so a wrong assumption is visible rather than folded in.
;
; bytes is the TRAFFIC. For a fill that is the bytes written; for a copy
; it is twice the rectangle, because every byte is read once and
; written once and the whole point of the exercise is memory bandwidth.
Procedure DpReport(*name, us.i, bytes.i)
  PutLabel(*name)
  PutS("  ")
  PutMs(us)
  PutS(" ms")
  If us > 0
    PutS("   ")
    PutFix2((bytes * 100) / us)
    PutS(" MB/s")
  EndIf
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; The headline. Two elapsed times over the same bytes, and the ratio
; between them printed to two places so a 1.9x cannot be read as a 2x.
Procedure DpCompare(*what, cpuUs.i, dmaUs.i)
  PutNl()
  PutLabel(*what)
  PutNl()
  If dmaUs < 1
    PutLabel("  the DMA pass measured zero microseconds - it did not run")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PutLabel("  hardware is ")
  PutFix2((cpuUs * 100) / dmaUs)
  PutS("x the CPU")
  PutNl()
  If cpuUs > dmaUs
    PutLabel("  saved ")
    PutMs(cpuUs - dmaUs)
    PutS(" ms")
  Else
    PutLabel("  COST ")
    PutMs(dmaUs - cpuUs)
    PutS(" ms - the hardware path is SLOWER here")
  EndIf
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DpBurstSweep - the same full-surface fill at several burst lengths.
;
; TI.BURST_LENGTH is the one parameter that materially changes
; throughput, and a default nobody has swept is a guess.
;
; THE CEILING IS DmaBurstMaxSafe() AND IS NOT WRITTEN DOWN HERE. It
; used to be a literal 8, justified by section 4.3's warning that "if a
; read burst is selected that is too large, the AXI read bus will be
; stalled... and possibly AXI lock up" (:5238-5240) plus the fact that
; channel 0's external FIFO is eight words. That was reasoning from a
; mechanism, and on 2026-08-26 pi4DmaGate.pi4's group F turned the
; warning into a measurement: a burst above 8 locked the bus and the
; deadman had to reset the board.
;
; So the number now comes from dma.pi4's #DMA_BURST_MAX_SAFE, which is
; the same cap DmaSetBurst() enforces as a refusal. Two consequences,
; and both are the point:
;
;   * THIS SWEEP CANNOT AIM A BUS-LOCKING TRANSFER AT THE FRAMEBUFFER.
;     It could not even if it tried - DmaSetBurst() would refuse.
;   * WHEN THE GATE NARROWS THE CEILING, THIS FOLLOWS AUTOMATICALLY.
;     There is no second copy of the number to drift out of step with
;     the first, which is exactly how "8" survived here for a day as a
;     value nobody could justify.
;
; Finding the ceiling is the GATE's job and it does it on a scratch
; buffer with the screen not involved. This program's job is to measure
; throughput inside it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DpBurstSweep(base.i, pitch.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define n.i
  Define t0.i
  Define us.i
  Define bytes.i
  Define save.i

  save  = DmaBurst()
  bytes = (w * 4) * h

  PutS("-- 4c. burst length sweep, the same full-surface fill each time")
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("ceiling is DmaBurstMaxSafe() = ")
  PrintDec(DmaBurstMaxSafe())
  PutS("   (measured, not chosen - see dma.pi4)")
  PutNl()

  n = 0
  While n <= DmaBurstMaxSafe()
    DmaSetBurst(n)
    t0 = Micros()
    If DmaFill32(base, pitch, w, h, $FF000000 + (n * 16)) = 0
      PutLabel("burst ")
      PrintDec(n)
      PutS("  REFUSED - ")
      PutS(DmaErrorText())
      PutNl()
    Else
      us = Micros() - t0
      PutLabel("burst ")
      PrintDec(n)
      PutS("   ")
      PutMs(us)
      PutS(" ms   ")
      If us > 0
        PutFix2((bytes * 100) / us)
        PutS(" MB/s")
      EndIf
      PutNl()
    EndIf
    If n = 0
      n = 1
    Else
      n = n * 2
    EndIf
  Wend

  DmaSetBurst(save)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 4d - THE DISPLAY PATH ITSELF, A AGAINST B
; ======================================================================
;  Everything above this point exercises dma.pi4 directly. This section
;  exercises THE WIRING - display.pi4's DisplayFillRect deciding to hand
;  a rectangle to the engine, computing its address and pitch, running
;  the cache sequence, and declining to auto-flush afterwards.
;
;  THAT WIRING HAS NO OTHER TEST AND CANNOT HAVE ONE ELSEWHERE.
;  pi4DmaGate.pi4 is the differential gate for dma.pi4 and it has no
;  framebuffer, no mailbox and no display.pi4 in its build; a Python
;  gate over tools/a64/a64_interp.py has a flat memory model and cannot
;  represent a cache at all, let alone a second bus master. The only
;  place the display path can be checked is on the surface it runs on,
;  which is here.
;
;  THE METHOD IS THE SAME ONE THE GATE USES: do the same rectangle
;  twice, once each way, and require the two results to be identical
;  byte for byte - over a window LARGER than the rectangle, so that
;  damage beside it counts as damage.
;
;  THE ORDER IS CPU FIRST, THEN DMA, and both passes start from a known
;  different background so that "the engine did nothing" cannot pass.
;  If the engine never wrote, its capture holds the background and the
;  CPU's capture holds the rectangle, and they differ - which is the
;  correct verdict for a path that silently declined.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; DpCapture - copy a window of the surface into a buffer, honestly.
;
; THE INVALIDATE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS IS A
; PROCEDURE. Reading pixels the engine wrote, with the caches on, is
; hazard B out of display.pi4's coherency section: the CPU may hold a
; stale clean line over them and will happily serve it. A capture that
; skipped this would read the CPU's own earlier picture back out of the
; cache and report that the two passes agreed - the single most
; convincing wrong answer this program could produce.
;
; PeekN, the unsigned 32-bit read, and PokeL to store: a bare Peek/Poke
; is eight bytes on this target and would take two pixels at a time,
; which is right by accident here and wrong the moment the window width
; is odd.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DpCapture(buf.i, base.i, pitch.i, x0.i, y0.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define r.i
  Define c.i
  Define s.i
  Define d.i

  DmaCacheRect(base + (y0 * pitch) + (x0 * 4), pitch, w * 4, h)

  d = buf
  r = 0
  While r < h
    s = (base + ((y0 + r) * pitch)) + (x0 * 4)
    c = 0
    While c < w
      PokeL(d, PeekN(s))
      s = s + 4
      d = d + 4
      c = c + 1
    Wend
    r = r + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; First differing byte between two captures, or -1. The OFFSET is the
; diagnosis: divide by 4 for the pixel, then by the window width for the
; row, and whether it lands inside the rectangle or in the eight-pixel
; margin says which of two completely different bugs it is.
Procedure.i DpCapDiff(a.i, b.i, bytes.i)
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < bytes
    If (PeekB(a + i) & $FF) <> (PeekB(b + i) & $FF)
      ProcedureReturn i
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

Procedure DpDisplayAB(base.i, pitch.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define bytes.i
  Define diff.i
  Define px.i
  Define col.i
  Define row.i
  Define opsBefore.i
  Define bg.i
  Define fg.i

  PutS("-- 4d. the DISPLAY PATH: DisplayFillRect with the engine off, then on")
  PutNl()

  If DisplayDmaLinked() = 0
    PutS("     SKIPPED - this build has #DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0, so display.pi4 has")
    PutNl()
    PutS("     no accelerated path to compare against. Nothing is wrong; this")
    PutNl()
    PutS("     program is simply not the build that tests the wiring.")
    PutNl()
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; The window has to fit on the surface. A board reporting a smaller
  ; mode than expected is a real possibility - this one reported
  ; 1824 x 984 under overscan - and running the A/B off the edge would
  ; compare two clipped rectangles and call them equal.
  If (#DP_AB_WINX + #DP_AB_WINW) > w Or (#DP_AB_WINY + #DP_AB_WINH) > h
    PutS("     SKIPPED - the surface is smaller than the 128 x 128 window this")
    PutNl()
    PutS("     comparison needs at (56,56). Not a failure; move the window.")
    PutNl()
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  bytes = (#DP_AB_WINW * #DP_AB_WINH) * 4
  bg    = DisplayRGB(20, 20, 60)
  fg    = DisplayRGB(240, 120, 0)

  ; ---- pass A: the CPU ----------------------------------------------
  ; The engine is switched off at run time, not unlinked, so the two
  ; passes differ in exactly one thing.
  DisplayUseDma(0)
  DisplayFillRect(#DP_AB_WINX, #DP_AB_WINY, #DP_AB_WINW, #DP_AB_WINH, bg)
  DisplayFillRect(#DP_AB_X, #DP_AB_Y, #DP_AB_W, #DP_AB_H, fg)
  DpCapture(#DP_CAP_A, base, pitch, #DP_AB_WINX, #DP_AB_WINY, #DP_AB_WINW, #DP_AB_WINH)

  ; ---- scrub, so a pass that draws nothing cannot agree --------------
  ; A DIFFERENT colour, not the background either pass will use. If the
  ; DMA pass declines and writes nothing, the capture holds THIS and the
  ; comparison fails, which is the honest outcome for a silent decline.
  DisplayFillRect(#DP_AB_WINX, #DP_AB_WINY, #DP_AB_WINW, #DP_AB_WINH, DisplayRGB(0, 200, 0))

  ; ---- pass B: the engine -------------------------------------------
  opsBefore = DisplayDmaOps()
  DisplayUseDma(1)
  DisplayFillRect(#DP_AB_WINX, #DP_AB_WINY, #DP_AB_WINW, #DP_AB_WINH, bg)
  DisplayFillRect(#DP_AB_X, #DP_AB_Y, #DP_AB_W, #DP_AB_H, fg)
  DpCapture(#DP_CAP_B, base, pitch, #DP_AB_WINX, #DP_AB_WINY, #DP_AB_WINW, #DP_AB_WINH)

  ; ---- DID THE ENGINE ACTUALLY DO IT? --------------------------------
  ; ASKED BEFORE THE PIXELS ARE COMPARED, because it is the question
  ; that decides what the comparison means. display.pi4's accelerated
  ; primitives fall back to the CPU whenever anything is not certain,
  ; and a fallback DRAWS THE RIGHT PICTURE - so a green comparison with
  ; a zero op count is a program that tested the CPU path twice.
  dpChecks = dpChecks + 1
  If DisplayDmaOps() = opsBefore
    dpFails = dpFails + 1
    PutS("  FAIL  the engine performed NO transfers during pass B, so the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("        comparison below is the CPU path against itself. Refusals so")
    PutNl()
    PutS("        far: ")
    PrintDec(DisplayDmaRefusals())
    PutS(", policy fallbacks: ")
    PrintDec(DisplayDmaFallbacks())
    PutNl()
    If DisplayDmaRefusals() > 0
      PutS("        the last refusal was: ")
      PutS(DmaErrorText())
      PutNl()
      PutS("        DmaSetBounds not pointed at the surface is the usual cause -")
      PutNl()
      PutS("        DisplayDmaBind() is the one call that does it.")
      PutNl()
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; ---- the comparison -------------------------------------------------
  diff = DpCapDiff(#DP_CAP_A, #DP_CAP_B, bytes)
  dpChecks = dpChecks + 1
  If diff < 0
    PutLabel("the two passes are byte-identical over the whole 128 x 128 window,")
    PutNl()
    PutLabel("margin included. ")
    PrintDec(DisplayDmaOps() - opsBefore)
    PutS(" transfer(s) went through the engine.")
    PutNl()
  Else
    dpFails = dpFails + 1
    px  = diff / 4
    col = px % #DP_AB_WINW
    row = px / #DP_AB_WINW
    PutS("  FAIL  the DMA pass differs from the CPU pass at window pixel (")
    PrintDec(col)
    PutS(",")
    PrintDec(row)
    PutS(")")
    PutNl()
    PutS("        cpu $")
    PutHexN(PeekN(#DP_CAP_A + (px * 4)) & $FFFFFFFF, 8)
    PutS("   dma $")
    PutHexN(PeekN(#DP_CAP_B + (px * 4)) & $FFFFFFFF, 8)
    PutNl()
    ; WHERE it is relative to the filled rectangle is most of the
    ; diagnosis and a person should not have to work it out on paper.
    If col < (#DP_AB_X - #DP_AB_WINX) Or row < (#DP_AB_Y - #DP_AB_WINY)
      PutS("        IN THE MARGIN - the engine wrote outside the rectangle it was")
      PutNl()
      PutS("        given. Suspect the stride, or a row count one too large.")
      PutNl()
    ElseIf col >= ((#DP_AB_X - #DP_AB_WINX) + #DP_AB_W) Or row >= ((#DP_AB_Y - #DP_AB_WINY) + #DP_AB_H)
      PutS("        IN THE MARGIN - the engine wrote outside the rectangle it was")
      PutNl()
      PutS("        given. Suspect the stride, or a row count one too large.")
      PutNl()
    Else
      PutS("        INSIDE the rectangle - the fill itself, or the cache sequence")
      PutNl()
      PutS("        around it. Check pi4DmaGate group G before suspecting this.")
      PutNl()
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; Leave the engine on. Section 5 draws with it deliberately.
  DisplayUseDma(1)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  SECTION 5 - THE PICTURE
;
;  Eight colour bars, every pixel of them placed by the DMA engine, and
;  one line of text placed by display.pi4's CPU glyph loop on top. The
;  point is not decoration: a fill that is fast and WRONG looks like a
;  fill that is fast, and the timings above cannot tell the difference.
;  Bars in the right places, in the right colours, with sharp edges, is
;  the check a person can make in one second.
;
;  WHAT WRONG LOOKS LIKE, so it is recognised rather than puzzled over:
;    * bars sheared diagonally  - the pitch is wrong, or the stride is
;      being applied instead of added. display.pi4:313-331 has the same
;      note from the CPU side.
;    * bars the wrong colour but in the right places - red and blue are
;      swapped, which is a firmware pixel-order question and is asked
;      and answered in Main() below, not a DMA question.
;    * the last bar wider than the others - integer division; the eighth
;      bar deliberately takes the remainder.
;    * one bar missing entirely - it was refused. The transcript says
;      which and why.
; ======================================================================
Procedure DpDrawBars(base.i, pitch.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define i.i
  Define bw.i
  Define x.i
  Define cols.i
  Define c.i
  Define bad.i

  bw  = w / 8
  bad = 0

  i = 0
  While i < 8
    ; The palette, built with DisplayRGB so the firmware's pixel order
    ; is honoured rather than assumed - the same call every other
    ; program here uses, and the reason DisplayColourSwap() exists.
    Select i
      Case 0 : c = DisplayRGB(255, 255, 255)
      Case 1 : c = DisplayRGB(255, 255,   0)
      Case 2 : c = DisplayRGB(  0, 255, 255)
      Case 3 : c = DisplayRGB(  0, 255,   0)
      Case 4 : c = DisplayRGB(255,   0, 255)
      Case 5 : c = DisplayRGB(255,   0,   0)
      Case 6 : c = DisplayRGB(  0,   0, 255)
      Case 7 : c = DisplayRGB( 24,  24,  32)
    EndSelect

    x    = i * bw
    cols = bw
    If i = 7
      cols = w - x                       ; the remainder, so nothing is
    EndIf                                ; left unpainted at the edge

    If DmaFill32(base + (x * 4), pitch, cols, h, c) = 0
      bad = bad + 1
      PutLabel("bar ")
      PrintDec(i)
      PutS(" refused - ")
      PutS(DmaErrorText())
      PutNl()
    EndIf
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  Chk("eight colour bars, every pixel placed by the engine", bad, 0)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  MAIN
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i Main()
  Define rc.i
  Define w.i
  Define h.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define bpp.i
  Define base.i
  Define size.i
  Define order.i
  Define shadowPitch.i
  Define shadowBytes.i
  Define bytes.i
  Define t0.i
  Define adv.i
  Define hz.i
  Define cpuFillUs.i
  Define dmaFillUs.i
  Define engineUs.i
  Define cleanUs.i
  Define cpuBlitUs.i
  Define dmaBlitUs.i
  Define presentUs.i
  Define y.i
  Define p.i
  Define n.i

  UartInit(115200)

  PutNl()
  PutS("=== pi4DmaProbe - stage 1, pixels moved by the SoC instead of the CPU ===")
  PutNl()
  PutS("The number to beat is 266.4 ms, measured on this board 2026-08-26 with")
  PutNl()
  PutS("the caches on, for a full-surface opaque fill by display.pi4's CPU loop.")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE CACHES
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  MmuBuildTables RETURNS 0 ON SUCCESS and a negative #MMU_ERR_* on
  ;  refusal (mmu.pi4:1044-1046). Testing it the other way round is a
  ;  mistake that has already cost this project a whole run, because
  ;  the output looks complete: the measurement quietly runs UNCACHED
  ;  and reports uncached numbers as cached ones. pi4NeonLiar.pi4:1505
  ;  carries the same warning.
  ;
  ;  THE MMU GOES OFF AGAIN BEFORE THIS RETURNS. Anvil is running
  ;  underneath and knows nothing about page tables.
  If #DP_MMU = 1
    PutS("MMU: building the identity map at $600000 ...")
    PutNl()
    If MmuBuildTables(#DP_TABLES) <> 0
      PutS("MMU: the tables would not build - running UNCACHED. The CPU numbers")
      PutNl()
      PutS("     below will be the 356.9 ms ones, and the ratio will FLATTER the")
      PutNl()
      PutS("     hardware. Say so if you quote them.")
      PutNl()
    Else
      MmuEnable()
      If MmuEnabled() = 1
        PutS("MMU: ON. DRAM is Normal Write-Back Cacheable, which is the")
        PutNl()
        PutS("     configuration the 266.4 ms was measured in.")
        PutNl()
      Else
        PutS("MMU: the enable did not take - running UNCACHED.")
        PutNl()
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Else
    PutS("MMU: off by #DP_MMU. These are the UNCACHED numbers and the CPU side")
    PutNl()
    PutS("     of every comparison is the 356.9 ms one, not the 266.4 ms one.")
    PutNl()
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE SURFACE
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  If MailboxInit() = 0
    PutS("!! MailboxInit failed, MailboxError ")
    PrintDec(MailboxError())
    PutNl()
    PutS("   This is a transport failure, not a graphics one. Nothing below can run.")
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  DisplayStandardTags()
  rc = DisplayInit(1920, 1080, 32)
  If DisplayReady() = 0
    PutS("!! DisplayInit returned ")
    PrintDec(rc)
    PutS(", DisplayLastError ")
    PrintDec(DisplayLastError())
    PutNl()
    PutS("   display.pi4:916-957 names every one of those codes. The usual cause")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   is no HDMI display connected when the board powered on.")
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 2
  EndIf

  ; Red and blue asked rather than assumed, exactly as anvil.pi4 and
  ; pi4NeonLiar.pi4 do. Tag $00040006 is GET_PIXEL_ORDER; 0 is BGR and
  ; 1 is RGB (Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h:373-374).
  order = -1
  If MailboxBegin($00040006, 4, 4) <> 0
    MailboxSetWord(0, 0)
    If MailboxSend() <> 0
      order = MailboxWord(0)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If order = 1
    DisplayColourSwap(1)
  Else
    DisplayColourSwap(0)
  EndIf

  w     = DisplayWidth()
  h     = DisplayHeight()
  pitch = DisplayPitch()
  bpp   = DisplayBytesPerPixel()
  base  = DisplayBase()
  size  = DisplaySize()

  PutS("-- 0. the surface, as the firmware reported it")
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("geometry        ")
  PrintDec(w)
  PutS(" x ")
  PrintDec(h)
  PutS(" x ")
  PrintDec(bpp * 8)
  PutS(" bpp")
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("pitch           ")
  PrintDec(pitch)
  PutS(" bytes per row")
  If pitch <> (w * bpp)
    PutS("   (NOT width*bpp - the linear present is not available)")
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("ARM base        $")
  PutHexN(base, 8)
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("VideoCore bus   $")
  PutHexN(DisplayBusBase(), 8)
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("size            ")
  PrintDec(size)
  PutS(" bytes")
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("as the engine will be told it   $")
  PutHexN(DmaBusAddr(base), 8)
  PutNl()

  ; The surface has to be inside the low gigabyte or the legacy engines
  ; cannot see it. display.pi4:2053 masks the firmware's bus address
  ; with $3FFFFFFF, so it always is - but the whole point of dma.pi4's
  ; HAZARD 1 is that this is the check nobody makes, so it is made.
  Chk("the framebuffer is inside the 1 GiB the engine can reach", DmaAddrOk(base, size), 1)

  ; EVERY PIXEL CALCULATION BELOW ASSUMES FOUR BYTES. DmaFill32() is a
  ; 32bpp entry point by name, the colour bars index columns with
  ; "x * 4", and the traffic figures are width * 4 * height. display.pi4
  ; will happily give a 16bpp surface (#DSP_EDEPTH accepts 16 or 32), so
  ; the assumption is checked rather than left to produce a picture that
  ; is half as wide as it should be and a rate that is twice what it
  ; really was. A 16bpp caller is served by DmaFillPattern32() with a
  ; doubled half-word; this program simply does not do that case.
  If bpp <> 4
    Chk("the surface is 32bpp - everything below assumes four bytes", bpp, 4)
    PutS("   Nothing below this line would be meaningful. Stopping.")
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()
    If #DP_MMU = 1
      If MmuEnabled() = 1
        MmuDisable()
      EndIf
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 4
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 1 - THE CONTROLLER
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  DpReportController()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  BRING dma.pi4 UP, POINTED AT THE SANDBOX
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 2. bringing up RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4")
  PutNl()

  ; Put the bias back to "unknown" so the probe re-derives it on this
  ; run rather than inheriting the constant. See the same call in
  ; pi4DmaGate.pi4 for the argument.
  DmaSetYLengthBias(-1)

  Chk("DmaSetScratch($700000, 512)", DmaSetScratch(#DP_SCRATCH, #DP_SCRATCH_BYTES), 1)
  If DmaError() <> #DMA_ERR_NONE
    PutDmaErr()
  EndIf

  ; The window starts as the SANDBOX, not the framebuffer. Section 3
  ; runs entirely inside it, so a rectangle-arithmetic bug in a test is
  ; refused instead of landing on the screen - or somewhere worse.
  Chk("DmaSetBounds(the 64 KiB sandbox)", DmaSetBounds(#DP_SAND, #DP_SAND_BYTES), 1)

  If DmaInit() = 0
    Chk("DmaInit()", 0, 1)
    PutDmaErr()
    PutS("   Nothing below this line can run.")
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()
    If #DP_MMU = 1
      If MmuEnabled() = 1
        MmuDisable()
      EndIf
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 3
  EndIf
  Chk("DmaInit()", 1, 1)

  PutLabel("channel                 ")
  PrintDec(DmaChannel())
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("burst length            ")
  PrintDec(DmaBurst())
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("YLENGTH source advance  ")
  adv = DmaCalibrateAdvance()
  PrintDec(adv)
  PutS(" bytes")
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("YLENGTH bias            ")
  PrintDec(DmaYLengthBias())
  If DmaYLengthBias() = 0
    PutS("    YLENGTH IS A PLAIN ROW COUNT on this part.")
  Else
    PutS("    YLENGTH IS ROWS MINUS ONE on this part.")
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  PutS("    Write that down. It is the one thing the vendor PDF does not say")
  PutNl()
  PutS("    for the legacy engine, and once it is known a program can state it")
  PutNl()
  PutS("    with DmaSetYLengthBias() and skip the probe.")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 3 - CORRECTNESS
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 3. correctness, entirely inside the 64 KiB sandbox at $780000")
  PutNl()
  DpTestFill()
  DpTestCopy()
  DpTestScroll()
  DpTestRefusals()
  PutNl()

  If dpFails <> 0
    PutS("!! Correctness failed. The timings below would be meaningless and the")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   framebuffer is deliberately left alone. Fix the failing line first.")
    PutNl()
    UartDrain()
    If #DP_MMU = 1
      If MmuEnabled() = 1
        MmuDisable()
      EndIf
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn dpFails
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 4 - THE TIMINGS
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  From here on the destination is the framebuffer, so the window
  ;  moves. This is the only place in the program that widens it, and
  ;  it happens after every correctness property has been proved.
  ;
  ;  THROUGH DisplayDmaBind() AND NOT DmaSetBounds() DIRECTLY. The two
  ;  are the same call with the same two numbers; the difference is that
  ;  DisplayDmaBind takes them from the firmware's own answer instead of
  ;  from two locals this program happens to be holding, and section 4d
  ;  below depends on the window being the surface. It is also the call
  ;  every other program will make, so it is the one that should be
  ;  under test - a public entry point no gate exercises is a public
  ;  entry point nobody knows is broken.
  rc = DisplayDmaBind()
  Chk("DisplayDmaBind() points the engine at the surface", rc, 1)
  If rc = 0
    PutDmaErr()
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; The auto-flush goes off for the whole timing section. display.pi4
  ; would otherwise clean 130,000 cache lines after every primitive,
  ; which is not what "how fast is a store loop" means.
  DisplayAutoFlush(0)

  bytes = (w * bpp) * h
  hz    = DmaCounterHz()

  PutS("-- 4a. the full-surface opaque fill - THE HEADLINE")
  PutNl()

  t0 = Micros()
  DisplayFillRect(0, 0, w, h, DisplayRGB(0, 0, 40))
  cpuFillUs = Micros() - t0
  DpReport("CPU, display.pi4 DspRowFill32 ", cpuFillUs, bytes)

  ; CLEAN AND INVALIDATE THE SURFACE BEFORE THE ENGINE WRITES IT, AND
  ; DO IT OUTSIDE THE TIMED REGION.
  ;
  ; The CPU fill above left the whole surface in the D-cache, dirty.
  ; If those lines were still there when the engine wrote DRAM, a later
  ; eviction would put the CPU's pixels back OVER the engine's - a
  ; corruption that appears seconds after the transfer, in bands, and
  ; looks like a DMA fault. dma.pi4's DmaFillPattern32 header names it.
  ;
  ; It is outside the timed region because it is an artefact of this
  ; program doing both things to one surface. A compositor that uses
  ; the engine does not first paint the same pixels with the CPU, so
  ; charging the hardware path for this would be inventing a cost.
  DmaCacheRange(base, size)

  t0 = Micros()
  rc = DmaFill32(base, pitch, w, h, DisplayRGB(0, 40, 0))
  dmaFillUs = Micros() - t0

  If rc = 0
    Chk("DmaFill32 over the whole surface", 0, 1)
    PutDmaErr()
  Else
    DpReport("DMA, dma.pi4 DmaFill32        ", dmaFillUs, bytes)

    ; The engine's own time, from CNTPCT_EL0 sampled either side of the
    ; ACTIVE poll inside the library. The difference between this and
    ; the figure above is the control-block setup and the completion
    ; poll, and a caller doing many small rectangles pays it every time.
    If hz > 0
      engineUs = (DmaLastTicks() * 1000000) / hz
      PutLabel("of which the engine itself     ")
      PutMs(engineUs)
      PutS(" ms   (the rest is control-block setup and the completion poll)")
      PutNl()
    EndIf
    DpCompare("FILL, full surface:", cpuFillUs, dmaFillUs)
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  4b. the blit, from a CPU-composed shadow surface
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  shadowPitch = w * bpp
  shadowBytes = shadowPitch * h

  PutS("-- 4b. the full-surface blit, from a shadow buffer at $1000000")
  PutNl()

  If DmaAddrOk(#DP_SHADOW, shadowBytes) = 0
    PutLabel("the shadow buffer is not reachable by this engine - skipped")
    PutNl()
  Else
    ; Compose something into the shadow with the CPU: one word per row,
    ; so a blit that repeated a row or dropped one would be visible on
    ; the screen afterwards rather than only in a number.
    y = 0
    While y < h
      p = #DP_SHADOW + (y * shadowPitch)
      n = 0
      While n < w
        PokeL(p, ((y & 255) << 8) | (n & 255))
        p = p + 4
        n = n + 1
      Wend
      y = y + 1
    Wend

    ; THE CPU BLIT GOES FIRST, BEFORE THE CACHE CLEAN, AND THE ORDER IS
    ; DELIBERATE. Cleaning the shadow buffer INVALIDATES it as well -
    ; "dc civac" - so a CPU blit run afterwards would start from a cold
    ; cache while the DMA path would not, and the comparison would be
    ; tilted by an artefact of the measurement. Run in this order both
    ; paths read the same 8.29 MB out of DRAM, which is what actually
    ; happens: the A72's L2 is a megabyte and the surface is eight, so
    ; nothing was ever going to be resident.
    t0 = Micros()
    DisplayBlit(#DP_SHADOW, shadowPitch, 0, 0, w, h)
    cpuBlitUs = Micros() - t0
    DpReport("CPU, display.pi4 DisplayBlit  ", cpuBlitUs, bytes * 2)

    ; THE CLEAN IS PART OF THE DMA PATH'S BILL AND IS TIMED SEPARATELY.
    t0 = Micros()
    DmaCacheRange(#DP_SHADOW, shadowBytes)
    cleanUs = Micros() - t0

    DmaCacheRange(base, size)

    t0 = Micros()
    rc = DmaCopy2D(base, pitch, #DP_SHADOW, shadowPitch, shadowPitch, h)
    dmaBlitUs = Micros() - t0

    If rc = 0
      Chk("DmaCopy2D over the whole surface", 0, 1)
      PutDmaErr()
    Else
      DpReport("DMA, dma.pi4 DmaCopy2D        ", dmaBlitUs, bytes * 2)
      PutLabel("plus the source cache clean    ")
      PutMs(cleanUs)
      PutS(" ms   (129,600 lines over 8.29 MB, and it is real)")
      PutNl()
      DpCompare("BLIT, DMA time only:", cpuBlitUs, dmaBlitUs)
      DpCompare("BLIT, DMA time plus the clean it needs:", cpuBlitUs, dmaBlitUs + cleanUs)
    EndIf

    ; The linear present. Only correct when the surface is contiguous,
    ; which means pitch = width * bpp; display.pi4's DspFlushSpan
    ; (:2316-2331) carves out the same special case with the same
    ; caveat. When it applies it is one control block for 8.29 MB with
    ; no stride to re-evaluate 1080 times.
    If pitch = shadowPitch
      DmaCacheRange(base, size)
      t0 = Micros()
      rc = DmaCopy(base, #DP_SHADOW, shadowBytes)
      presentUs = Micros() - t0
      If rc = 0
        Chk("DmaCopy, the linear present", 0, 1)
        PutDmaErr()
      Else
        DpReport("DMA, one linear DmaCopy       ", presentUs, bytes * 2)
        DpCompare("PRESENT, linear versus the 2D copy above:", dmaBlitUs, presentUs)
      EndIf
    Else
      PutLabel("the linear present is skipped - the pitch is not width*bpp, so")
      PutNl()
      PutLabel("the surface is not one contiguous run and DmaCopy would be wrong")
      PutNl()
    EndIf
  EndIf
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  4c. the burst sweep
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  DpBurstSweep(base, pitch, w, h)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  4d. the display path, A against B
  ;
  ;  IT COMES AFTER EVERY TIMING AND NOT BEFORE. Sections 4a and 4b use
  ;  DisplayFillRect and DisplayBlit as the CPU REFERENCE, and the whole
  ;  comparison would be meaningless if those had quietly gone through
  ;  the engine. DisplayUseDma() defaults to OFF for exactly that reason
  ;  and this is the first thing in the program that turns it on.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  DpDisplayAB(base, pitch, w, h)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  SECTION 5 - THE PICTURE
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- 5. the picture")
  PutNl()
  DmaCacheRange(base, size)
  DpDrawBars(base, pitch, w, h)

  ; INVALIDATE BEFORE THE CPU DRAWS OVER THE ENGINE'S WORK.
  ;
  ; Added 2026-08-27. The clean above happens BEFORE the bars, which
  ; deals with dirty lines the CPU left behind; it does nothing about
  ; the other direction. Between that clean and the text below, the
  ; surface is Normal cacheable memory that this part read-allocates, so
  ; the A72 is architecturally free to pull lines in speculatively while
  ; the engine is painting DRAM underneath. A glyph write into such a
  ; line is a read-modify-write of the STALE picture, and DisplayFlush
  ; then cleans that stale picture back out over the bars - as a
  ; rectangle of old pixels around every character, which reads as a
  ; text-rendering fault rather than a coherency one.
  ;
  ; It costs one civac per 64 bytes of surface and it removes a whole
  ; class of "the bars looked wrong near the text" report.
  DmaCacheRange(base, size)

  ; One line of CPU text over the top, so both paths can be seen on one
  ; screen. It goes through display.pi4 unchanged; the surface is the
  ; same surface and neither path knows about the other.
  ;
  ; THE ENGINE IS SWITCHED OFF FOR THESE TWO LINES so that "placed by
  ; the CPU" is true. DisplayTextClear draws glyph cells, every one of
  ; them far below DisplayDmaMin(), so they would take the CPU path
  ; anyway - but a caption that depends on a threshold staying where it
  ; is today is a caption that will be a lie one tuning run from now.
  DisplayUseDma(0)
  DisplayTextClear(16, 16, "pi4DmaProbe - these bars were placed by the DMA engine", DisplayRGB(255, 255, 255))
  DisplayTextClear(16, 40, "this line was placed by the CPU, through display.pi4", DisplayRGB(200, 200, 200))
  DisplayFlush()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  WHAT THE SEAM ACTUALLY DID. Printed unconditionally, because the
  ;  one thing a caller cannot see from a correct picture is which path
  ;  drew it.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("-- the display seam")
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("dma linked into display.pi4  ")
  PrintDec(DisplayDmaLinked())
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("transfers through the engine ")
  PrintDec(DisplayDmaOps())
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("policy fallbacks to the CPU  ")
  PrintDec(DisplayDmaFallbacks())
  PutS("   (rectangles under DisplayDmaMin = ")
  PrintDec(DisplayDmaMinGet())
  PutS(" pixels)")
  PutNl()
  PutLabel("refusals by the engine       ")
  PrintDec(DisplayDmaRefusals())
  If DisplayDmaRefusals() > 0
    PutS("   <- read this: ")
    PutS(DmaErrorText())
  EndIf
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE TALLY
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("=== ")
  PrintDec(dpChecks - dpFails)
  PutS(" of ")
  PrintDec(dpChecks)
  PutS(" checks passed, ")
  PrintDec(dpFails)
  PutS(" failed ===")
  PutNl()
  PutS("Look at the screen now: eight colour bars, sharp edges, two lines of")
  PutNl()
  PutS("text. Anvil is about to print over the top of it - that is Anvil.")
  PutNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; Hand Anvil back the address space it thinks it has.
  If #DP_MMU = 1
    If MmuEnabled() = 1
      MmuDisable()
      PutS("MMU: off again - Anvil gets an untranslated machine back.")
      PutNl()
      UartDrain()
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn dpFails
EndProcedure
